\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\"],\n    \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter MS2500 with converter MV110; figures show sealing of compact and separate versions\"\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"converter\", \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\"]},\n    {\"role\": \"meter body (sensor)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"]}\n  ],\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"MS2500\", ...},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MV110\", ...}\n    ]\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, should MS2500/MV110 be separate models or components? They're converter + meter body forming one meter type \"MS2500 / MV110\". The figures call it \"water meter MS2500 / MV110\". So the type designation is the pair. I'd put type_designations: [\"MS2500\", \"MV110\"] and components for each. Not model_family — they're not variants of each other; model_family is for model variants. The label shows variants like \"MS2500-P80-B1A2B\" and \"MV110-B1B4B1B1B0A1\" — these are order-code variants. Are those model variants? The label example shows a specific configuration (Dn 80 etc.). I think the cert likely covers multiple diameters (earlier parts would show the flow tables). In this fragment, only the Dn=80 example appears.\n\nI'll skip model_family since this fragment doesn't clearly define model variants... Actually, wait. The label example shows concrete values: Q3=160 m³/h for Dn 80. But that's one example. Without the other parts, I can't build the matrix. I'll treat the label values cautiously.\n\nShould I extract label characteristics at all? Let me weigh: The instruction says extract content present. The label description is present. Values like IP 68 (MS2500), IP 67 (MV110), power supply 100-240VAC 44-66 Hz, temperature -25+55°C, Q3 160 m³/h, Q3/Q1 100 — these are legitimate instrument attributes shown on the label. I think extracting them adds value and matches the \"matrix detection\" spirit. But which level? They're shown for a specific example configuration, not universally. I could put them in matrix_tables as \"label example\" tables, preserving rows exactly. That's the safest: preserve the label tables in matrix_tables with description.\n\nOption A: matrix_tables with two entries:\n1. \"MS2500 label layout example\" — columns/rows from the label table.\n2. \"MV110 label layout example\" — same.\n\nThe label tables are key-value grids. Rows as objects: {\"field\": \"Dn\", \"value\": \"80\", \"field_2\": \"Pclass\", \"value_2\": \"MAP16\"}. Hmm, awkward. Alternatively rows: {\"Mod\": \"MS2500-P80-B1A2B\"}, {\"s/n\": ...}... Not clean.\n\nOption B: Extract meaningful attributes into characteristics (type_level), e.g.:\n- ingress_protection_rating (converter MV110): 67 → but which device? MS2500 has IP68, MV110 IP67.\n- Using components' characteristics? Schema: components[] have \"characteristics{}\". So I can attach label-derived characteristics to components:\n  - component MS2500 (meter body): ip_degree 68, nominal_diameter example 80 mm, pressure_class MAP16 (max admissible pressure 16 bar?), lining material P.P., electrodes 3-AISI316L, flanges EN1092.\n  - component MV110 (converter): ip_degree 67, power_supply_voltage 100-240 V, power_supply_frequency 44-66 Hz, accuracy_class 1, environmental_class \"O\", temperature range -25 to +55 °C, q3 160 m³/h, q3_q1_ratio 100, electromagnetic_environment \"B, O, E1, E2\".\n\nThat's fairly rich and faithful to the fragment. The concern: these are example label contents; e.g., Q3=160 m³/h is specific to Dn80. Ka=+1.2950 is the meter factor example. \"Δp XX\" and \"Tclass TXX\" are placeholders (XX), so omit those (rule 8: don't fabricate; XX is placeholder).\n\nGiven rule 4's emphasis, I'll include model_level? No — the variation axis here isn't model. I'll put them at component level characteristics with structured values.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n    \"page_total\": 16\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\", \"MV110\"]\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ISOIL Industria\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Cinisello B. (MI)\", \"Italy\"],\n      \"website\": \"www.isoil.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Hemina Spa (ISOIL production center)\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"35044 Montagnana - Padova\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"Flow sensor / meter body\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"],\n      \"characteristics\": {\n        \"type_level\": {\n          \"nominal_diameter\": {\"value\": 80, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"},\n          \"ingress_protection\": {\"value\": 68, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n          \"max_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", ...}\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWait — schema for components: `role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}`. characteristics presumably same shape as top-level characteristics (type_level etc.). I'll use a simple flat object? The schema says `characteristics{}` — ambiguous. Top-level characteristics has type_level. For components, I'll use `characteristics: {\"type_level\": {...}}` to be consistent.\n\nHmm, but MAP16: \"Max. admissible pressure\" callout; label shows \"Pclass MAP16\" → MAP = Maximum Admissible Pressure = 16 bar. Converting \"MAP16\" to 16 bar is a small inference; defensible. \"Dn 80\" — nominal diameter 80 (mm implied; DN80). I'll use value 80, unit \"mm\"? DN is dimensionless-ish; unit_symbol \"mm\" is standard for DN. I'll use unit_symbol \"mm\".\n\nKa +1.2950: meter factor (calibration factor). \"Meter Factor\" callout, label \"Ka +1.2950\". I could record meter_factor value \"+1.2950\"... it's an example value. Maybe skip; it's per-unit serial-specific. Actually Ka is typically fixed per meter type/size... I'll include as meter_factor value 1.2950? The + sign su