O, T... hmm. R49-2? For hot water meters? Classes: T50, T30, T10... Actually OIML R49-2 defines temperature classes for cold water meters: T30 (0.1–30°C), T50 (0.1–50°C), T70, T90... and \"T\" classes; also \"Tb\" classes for warm water. Class \"O\"? Hmm, R49-1 2014 ed.: temperature class \"O\" means 0.1°C to 55°C? Hmm, not sure. Actually OIML R49-1 (Edition 2014) Table: Temperature class O: MAT +55°C... Hmm hmm. Let me not overthink: record environmental_temperature_class: \"O\" and operating_temperature_range {min:-25, max:55} °C. Hmm wait, \"O (-25+55)°C\" — hmm, could be class O covering -25 to +55? Actually R49-2 (2013): water meters temperature classes: T30, T50, T70, T90 for cold; and classes... hmm \"O\"? Hmm, actually R49-2:2013 Table 1: Temperature classes for cold water meters: T30: 0.1–30; T50: 0.1–50; T70: 0.1–70; T90: 0.1–90. And \"Tb\" for... Hmm. But there's also \"-25+55\" which looks like R49 class \"O\" (outdoor)? In WELMEC guide / EN 14154, environmental classes: class O (outdoor): -25°C/+55°C; class A: +5/+55; class B: +5/+70? Yes! EN 14154 / OIML R49-2:2013 \"climatic environmental classes\": Class O: lower temperature -25°C, upper +55°C; Class A: -5? hmm. Actually OIML R49-2 Table: \"Class O: -25 °C to +55 °C\". I'm fairly confident: O = outdoor, -25/+55. Good, so ETR \"O (-25+55)°C\" = environmental temperature range class O. Record both: environmental_class \"O\" and temperature range.\n\n- EEC B,O,E1,E2: R49 electromagnetic environment classes E1, E2 (and B, O are... hmm, maybe \"B\" = ... Actually EEC could combine mechanical (B?) Hmm. OIML R49-2 Table 2: Electromagnetic environment classes E1, E2, E3, E4, E5. Mechanical environment classes M1, M2, M3. Hmm \"B\" might be from EN14154 mechanical class B? EN 14154-3? Hmm. Whatever — record as-is: \"B,O,E1,E2\" string. The callout says \"Electromagnetic environment\". I'll record electromagnetic_environment_classes: \"B, O, E1, E2\". Or split? Keep string.\n\n- POWER 100-240VAC/44-66 → voltag